WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.269
-Our first guest tonight is
a legendary Emmy- and Academy

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:05.237
award-winning actress
you know from films

00:00:05.237 --> 00:00:07.239
such as "Monster's Ball,"
"Die Another Day,"

00:00:07.239 --> 00:00:08.541
and the X-Men franchise.

00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:11.177
She both stars in and makes
her directorial debut

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:13.680
with "Bruised," which is
streaming now on Netflix.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:14.814
Let's take a look.

00:00:32.831 --> 00:00:34.567
-Immaculate sent you?

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:35.769
-Yeah.

00:00:38.404 --> 00:00:41.841
-You used to be Jackie justice,
right?

00:00:41.841 --> 00:00:43.242
-Still am.

00:00:43.242 --> 00:00:45.344
-Oh, yeah.

00:00:45.344 --> 00:00:47.246
We'll see.

00:00:47.246 --> 00:00:49.816
-Please welcome to the show
Halle Berry, everybody.

00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:51.350
Hello, Halle.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:54.287
-Hello!

00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:55.788
-It's so lovely to see you
again.

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:57.590
-Hi, Seth.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:00.193
-I'm so happy to have you
on the show.

00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:02.461
I wish you were here,
but you have a wonderful reason

00:01:02.461 --> 00:01:03.930
for doing this remotely.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.432
Tonight at the People's Choice
Award, you are going to win

00:01:06.432 --> 00:01:09.202
the Icon Award -- be presented
with the Icon Award.

00:01:09.202 --> 00:01:10.936
Congratulations.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:10.936 --> 00:01:12.838
-Thank you, thank you.

00:01:12.838 --> 00:01:15.308
Otherwise, of course,
I would be right there.

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:16.943
-A little bittersweet for me.

00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:18.177
I was runner-up.

00:01:18.177 --> 00:01:20.580
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.449
Last year, too, so it's
really getting hard.

00:01:23.449 --> 00:01:25.985
-I'll share it with you.
-Thank you.

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:28.655
First of all, it just must be so
nice to go to an award show

00:01:28.655 --> 00:01:31.657
knowing in advance that you are
going to be receiving an award.

00:01:31.657 --> 00:01:32.759
You don't have to be a nominee

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:34.160
through the first half
of the night.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.429
-[ Laughs ]
That is a good feeling.

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:38.865
But see, now you know
you have to have a speech.

00:01:38.865 --> 00:01:40.532
You know you have to
have something to say.

00:01:40.532 --> 00:01:42.768
When you don't know if you're
gonna win, you can think,

00:01:42.768 --> 00:01:45.471
"I don't really have to think of
something to say

00:01:45.471 --> 00:01:48.341
'cause I might not win,"
so, you know.

00:01:48.341 --> 00:01:51.344
-That's true. You cannot do that
move tonight where you get up

00:01:51.344 --> 00:01:53.379
and you go, "Oh, my God,
I didn't write anything!"

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:56.049
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.049 --> 00:02:00.019
Very exciting, as well -- I'm
even maybe more jealous of this.

00:02:00.019 --> 00:02:01.454
Not that you're winning
the award,

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:06.259
but your presenter is Cardi B,
who did a song on the album.

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:08.193
Collaborating with her
on this film,

00:02:08.193 --> 00:02:10.630
was that the first time
you'd gotten to know her?

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:14.300
-Yes. I mean, I knew of her
and I've been a fan of her

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.035
since she came on the scene.

00:02:16.035 --> 00:02:17.136
I mean, who hasn't?

00:02:17.136 --> 00:02:19.038
I mean, she's just been
a powerhouse.

00:02:19.038 --> 00:02:21.140
But it was our first time
collaborating

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:25.044
and it's just been a dream to
work with her

00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:28.314
and put the whole album together
and just support these ladies

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.783
and have their sound be
a part of the film.

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:34.821
It's just been a dream-come-true
experience.

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:38.424
-The character you play is
what we've all come to

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:40.959
expect from you -- it's this
wonderful acting performance.

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:43.295
You play this broken,
very layered character,

00:02:43.295 --> 00:02:46.332
but this is your first time
behind the camera.

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:48.468
I heard that
you don't actually like

00:02:48.468 --> 00:02:50.803
watching yourself on camera.

00:02:50.803 --> 00:02:53.806
Does that make directing
yourself almost impossible?

00:02:53.806 --> 00:02:55.041
[ Laughter ]

00:02:55.041 --> 00:02:57.509
-Not impossible to direct
but impossible to watch.

00:02:57.509 --> 00:03:00.713
The edit was gnarly.
[ Laughs ]

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:02.548
I mean, I really got tired of

00:03:02.548 --> 00:03:04.817
looking at my face
and looking at myself.

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:07.320
And I kept trying to cut myself
out, and my editor was like,

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.322
"You know, you have to stay
in this movie.

00:03:09.322 --> 00:03:10.923
You're the protagonist."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:10.923 --> 00:03:13.459
I kept saying, "But you don't
really need me here.

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:15.060
You don't need that shot on me
here."

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.762
It's really very hard for me
to watch myself.

00:03:16.762 --> 00:03:18.698
-Were you surprised?

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:21.634
Having to revisit
your performances each day,

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:24.870
were you surprised at which ones
worked and which ones didn't

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:26.205
once you got a chance
to see them?

00:03:26.205 --> 00:03:28.074
Or was it, as an actor,
did you think in your head,

00:03:28.074 --> 00:03:30.142
"Oh, this'll be the one we use"?

00:03:30.142 --> 00:03:31.844
-A little bit of both.

00:03:31.844 --> 00:03:33.146
Because of the nature
of the movie,

00:03:33.146 --> 00:03:34.514
it was a lower-budget movie,

00:03:34.514 --> 00:03:37.083
so it's not like I had a lot
of choices to choose from,

00:03:37.083 --> 00:03:38.384
if I'm telling you the truth.

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:40.820
I mean, some shots,
we only got two takes,

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:44.390
so I had one or two
to choose from.

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:49.028
So, you know, really didn't have
that problem necessarily.

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:51.097
-As someone who doesn't like
going back

00:03:51.097 --> 00:03:52.965
and watching yourself,
does that mean --

00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:54.700
Do you ever revisit
your old work?

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:56.702
Do you ever watch
old Halle Berry movies?

00:03:56.702 --> 00:03:58.838
-No.
Sometimes when I'm watching,

00:03:58.838 --> 00:04:01.340
I'm scrolling through television
and one comes up.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:03.342
I literally am like,
"Aaah, God!"

00:04:03.342 --> 00:04:05.277
[ Laughter ]
"No, get it off!"

00:04:05.277 --> 00:04:07.346
No, I really -- it's painful.

00:04:07.346 --> 00:04:10.816
No, I don't. I see them --
We do a press screening

00:04:10.816 --> 00:04:12.585
so we can talk about it
at the junket,

00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:15.788
I go to the premiere,
and then that's it.

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:18.857
I never see them again,
except this one.

00:04:18.857 --> 00:04:20.526
-[ Laughs ]
Yeah.

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:23.329
I heard you brought
your 13-year-old daughter

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:26.098
to watch sort of
early cuts of the film.

00:04:26.098 --> 00:04:31.738
Was her eye helpful for you
and how did she react?

00:04:31.738 --> 00:04:35.374
-Yeah, I wanted to show her
because this movie took almost

00:04:35.374 --> 00:04:37.643
three years of my life
and she wanted to see, like,

00:04:37.643 --> 00:04:42.782
"What have you been doing all
this time with your time, Mom?"

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:45.484
So I took her to see it,
and unfortunately,

00:04:45.484 --> 00:04:47.553
I was doing a watch-down and
the color wasn't quite right

00:04:47.553 --> 00:04:49.956
and she only got to see the two
scenes that I was most nervous

00:04:49.956 --> 00:04:52.124
about, which were the two
love scenes in the movie

00:04:52.124 --> 00:04:54.827
because those were two scenes
that could tell us very clearly

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:56.262
where we were
with our color grade.

00:04:56.262 --> 00:04:59.698
And so that's all she got to see
and at the end, she was like,

00:04:59.698 --> 00:05:01.801
"So, that's what you've been
doing for three years?"

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:05.171
[ Laughter ]
"Great. Great, Mom."

00:05:05.171 --> 00:05:08.574
-"I need a mom!"
-I know.

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:09.742
-You're gallivanting around
doing love scenes.

00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:13.379
-And one that's not confused.
Yeah.

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:16.014
-Another thing that has been
reported on a great deal,

00:05:16.014 --> 00:05:18.584
you know, you play this sort of
an MMA-style fighter

00:05:18.584 --> 00:05:20.252
who's returning to the ring.

00:05:20.252 --> 00:05:23.723
You broke your ribs, real-life
broke your ribs very early

00:05:23.723 --> 00:05:27.626
in filming and toughed it out,
which I imagine is probably

00:05:27.626 --> 00:05:29.495
because you're an impossible
director to work with.

00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:30.930
You have no respect
for your actors.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.098
[ Laughter ]

00:05:32.098 --> 00:05:33.365
-None, especially this one.

00:05:33.365 --> 00:05:35.568
I was like, "Girl, tough that
[bleep] out."

00:05:35.568 --> 00:05:37.603
[ Laughter ]

00:05:37.603 --> 00:05:40.106
"I paid you a lot of money to be
here, come on, turn it out."

00:05:40.106 --> 00:05:42.308
[ Laughter ]
No.

00:05:42.308 --> 00:05:45.043
I had by that time,
after 2 1/2 years of training,

00:05:45.043 --> 00:05:48.314
I felt like a fighter and I had
the fighting spirit inside me,

00:05:48.314 --> 00:05:50.649
and quitting is just never what
fighters do.

00:05:50.649 --> 00:05:52.919
I mean, they fight with
dislocated shoulders,

00:05:52.919 --> 00:05:55.854
broken bones,
torn this, torn that,

00:05:55.854 --> 00:05:58.090
and just quitting
is not an option.

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:01.360
It's gonna take a referee
to say stop, you know?

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:04.597
-And I guess for the method
actor performance,

00:06:04.597 --> 00:06:07.065
it was probably maybe even
a little helpful to walk around

00:06:07.065 --> 00:06:08.801
with a busted-up rib.

00:06:08.801 --> 00:06:10.603
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:10.603 --> 00:06:12.138
Mm, no, not so much.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:12.138 --> 00:06:14.507
Couldn't breathe.
No, it wasn't so good.

00:06:14.507 --> 00:06:16.509
-Yeah.
Look, I twisted my ankle once

00:06:16.509 --> 00:06:18.277
and we canceled the show
for the whole week, so...

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:19.478
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, I get it.

00:06:19.478 --> 00:06:23.549
-No judgment, Halle.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:23.549 --> 00:06:26.085
You obviously did a lot of fight
training for other films,

00:06:26.085 --> 00:06:28.153
"John Wick" being an example.

00:06:28.153 --> 00:06:31.056
Did you have to do -- Like, did
you have a nice starting point

00:06:31.056 --> 00:06:34.494
going into this or was it
building up back from zero?

00:06:34.494 --> 00:06:36.896
-I had a good starting point
with "John Wick."

00:06:36.896 --> 00:06:41.300
I'd never worked harder until I
started to train for "Bruised."

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:46.639
I had to do gun training
and some basic martial arts,

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:49.475
but with "Bruised," I had to
learn six different disciplines

00:06:49.475 --> 00:06:51.377
that really weren't a part of
my "John Wick" training.

00:06:51.377 --> 00:06:54.747
So, physically, I was
in a great shape to start

00:06:54.747 --> 00:06:57.683
because I worked really hard
on that.

00:06:57.683 --> 00:06:59.886
But all the things that I had to
learn to look like

00:06:59.886 --> 00:07:03.555
a real MMA fighter and get all
that technical stuff in my body,

00:07:03.555 --> 00:07:05.791
I just had to put in the work
and time to learn it.

00:07:05.791 --> 00:07:07.527
-Well, it certainly shows.

00:07:07.527 --> 00:07:09.495
Congratulations so much
on the film

00:07:09.495 --> 00:07:11.264
and congratulations tonight.

00:07:11.264 --> 00:07:12.498
I do want to let you go
so you can start

00:07:12.498 --> 00:07:14.700
working on your speech.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.869
-Thank you.
I'm gonna share it with you.

00:07:16.869 --> 00:07:18.103
-Alright, thank you so much.

00:07:18.103 --> 00:07:20.105
I will be watching,
and I would like you to --

00:07:20.105 --> 00:07:21.573
Seth Adam Meyers.

00:07:21.573 --> 00:07:23.075
If you can just use the whole
name, that'd be wonderful.

00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:25.210
-Seth Adam Meyers. Okay, got it.

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:26.679
-Halle Berry, everybody!

00:07:26.679 --> 00:07:28.881
"Bruised" streaming now
on Netflix.

